From 8a6cdb9617b6bd51a4bff20574dd0cf23e1b1da2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:42:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Converter vignetting: calibrate a lens converter's corner
 falloff from a flat-field photo
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A lens converter (e.g. the GT-2 tele converter on a GR IIIx, shot without the
camera's crop) darkens the corners far more than the bare lens's profile knows,
and the manual Vignetting slider can't follow its steep falloff.

- develop: Optics gains converter_k (radial gain 1 + k0·r² + … + k4·r¹⁰, r over
  the half diagonal) and converter_amount (0..200, control
  optics.converterAmount). Both are left out of the stored JSON while unused,
  so existing settings serialize exactly as before; the merge JSON states them,
  so presets / copy / sync of Optics carry and clear them. Coefficients from
  outside are kept finite and bounded.
- pipeline: Warp applies the converter gain (capped at 4 stops) with the manual
  and embedded vignetting, independent of the lens-profile switch and off with
  the optics section; the GPU warp kernel evaluates the same formula
  (WP_N 65 -> 71).
- engine: flat_field fits the residual falloff of a flat-field photo
  (scene-linear, after the lens profile; median per radial bin, black cut-off
  left out and reported; Householder least squares) and refuses clipped, dark
  or non-flat photos with a reason. Command optics.fitConverterVignette {id?}.
- ui: Optics ▸ Converter Vignetting dropdown (None, saved profiles, Create from
  Selected Flat-Field Photo…, Manage Profiles…) with an Amount slider; the
  measurement runs in the background and opens a dialog to name the profile;
  profiles live in the app settings (converterProfiles); app.converterProfiles
  for scripts. Simplified and Traditional Chinese strings.
